From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: valentin rameau <valentin.rameau@outlook.fr>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 17:09:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 24/33] Run the APU on a second core, paced by guest cycles

The SPC700 and S-DSP are a physically separate chip -- own crystal, own
SRAM, four 8-bit latches as the only link to the 5A22 -- and emulating
them on the game thread cost 5.7 ms of a 16.6 ms frame on the Vita.

The game thread now publishes one target per frame (the guest cycle at
the end of the frame it just completed) and a worker walks the SPC to
that ceiling on another core. Determinism is preserved because the
worker is paced by GUEST cycles, never wall-clock: port writes carry
guest-cycle stamps, and writes issued during frame N+1 necessarily
carry stamps at or beyond the ceiling, so which APU cycle each event
lands on does not depend on how far the worker happens to have got.

The advance is sliced (rtl_apu_worker_slice) because an unsliced one
holds RtlApuLock for a third of a frame and every port write and audio
callback would wait behind it. apu_runToGuestCycle already returns
false on exhausting max_cycles with the ceiling unchanged, so slicing
needs no new APU code and cannot change what the SPC computes.

The interp bridge's pacing flushes had to go, not merely be left
redundant: they would do the work on the game thread and push the SPC
past the ceiling the worker is bounded by. The flush is split into
PACING (skipped in worker mode) and RENDEZVOUS (the four APU-port bus
accesses, kept -- there the guest really is waiting on the chip).

Verified with the audio oracle on PC over 2,000 frames: worker on and
worker off are BYTE-IDENTICAL, 1,944 of 1,999 frames non-silent, and
the worker did the advancing (52,368 slices). x86 is strongly ordered,
so this establishes the schedule, not thread safety on Cortex-A9 --
which is why every access, including the uint64_t ceiling, is under
RtlApuLock.

Measured on device: -5,835 us/frame, 47.9 -> 59.5 FPS, late=0.

Also: hoist mod_audio's "any active voice" test out of its per-frame
loop. It is linked into the Vita build and read, clamped and wrote back
every sample of every block with nothing playing -- 281 us per block on
the audio thread, all of it inside the RtlApuLock hold.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
